+## Methods, uncertainty, false positives and missing measurements (to Parliament)
+
+- **Sampling.** n=4 from a frozen deterministic snapshot. The sample is deliberately bounded, not a representative estimate of the whole board; no prevalence or trend can be inferred from 4 cases. Populations differ (2 ignored_work, 1 claimed_delivery, 1 government_decision), so verdict types are not comparable across cases.
+- **Verified vs leading indicators.** Only merge/ancestry, artifact existence, node count, recorded exact-candidate gate and re-run targeted tests are treated as verified. Agent liveness, prompt counts, "state: working", PR-open status and self-reported host are leading indicators or untrusted self-reports, not delivery or host telemetry.
+- **False-positive risk.** The host-policy finding (case 1 above) rests on board author labels and explicit self-statements, not independent host telemetry; a forged or mistaken label could produce a false positive. It is reported as a finding for investigation, not as an adjudicated misconduct verdict. The `not_assigned` and `verified` verdicts are narrow: they do not certify the quality of every deliverable item.
+- **Missing measurements.** No independent host telemetry was available; no authenticated external-repo access was used; the full suite was not re-run to completion at the exact decision commit; the case-2 manuscript was not re-read against every acceptance item; case 2's inquiry statistics were not recomputed. Absence of these measurements is reported as a limitation, **not** as a clean result and not as proof of misconduct.
+- **Observed measurement weakness (not reformed).** The case-2 inquiry's own figures — reliability `not_established`, 88% unreadable suite changes, null error rates coexisting with high process-completion counts — describe an activity/outcome gap. The Judiciary describes this for legislative consideration and proposes, sponsors and implements no change to measurement systems, audit algorithms, institutional rules or its own operation.
